\fs20\
\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An NSEvent object, or simply an \i event\i0 , contains information about an input action such as a mouse click or a key down. The Application Kit associates each such user action with a window, reporting the event to the application that created the window. The NSEvent object contains pertinent information about each event, such as where the mouse was located or which character was typed. As the application receives events, it temporarily places them in a buffer called the \i event queue\i0 . When the application is ready to process an event, it takes one from the queue.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSEvents are typically passed up the application\'27s \i responder chain\i0 , a series of objects that stand in line for event messages and untargeted action messages, as described in the NSResponder class specification. When the NSApplication object retrieves an event from the event queue, it dispatches the event to the appropriate NSWindow by invoking \b sendEvent:\b0 . The NSWindow then passes the event to its first responder in an event message such as \b mouseDown:\b0  or \b keyDown:\b0 , and the event gets passed on up the responder chain until some object handles it. In the case of a mouse-down event, a \b mouseDown:\b0  message is sent to the NSView where the user clicked the mouse; if it doesn\'27t handle the event itself, the NSView relays the message to its next responder.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Most events follow this same path: from the windowing system to the application\'27s event queue, and from there to the appropriate objects in the application. Though it rarely need do so, an application can also create an event from scratch and insert it into the event queue for distribution, or send it directly to its destination in an event message. The newly created events can be added to the event queue by invoking NSWindow\'27s (or NSApplication\'27s) \b postEvent:atStart:\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 While most events are distributed automatically through the responder chain, sometimes an object needs to retrieve events explicitly\'d0for example, while handling mouse-dragged events. NSWindow and NSApplication define the method \b nextEventMatchingMask:untilDate:inMode:dequeue:\b0 , which allows an object to retrieve events of specific types. The nature of the retrieved event can then be ascertained by invoking NSEvent instance methods\'d0\b type\b0 , \b window\b0 , and so on. All types of events are associated with an NSWindow; the \b window\b0  method returns this object. The location of a mouse event within the window\'27s coordinate system is given by \b locationInWindow\b0 , and the time of the event by \b timestamp\b0 . The \b modifierFlags\b0  method returns an indication of which modifier keys (Command, Control, Shift, and so on) the user held down while the event occurred.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The \b type\b0  method returns an NSEventType value that identifies the sort of event. The different types of events fall into five groups:\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx3376 \tx3753 \fs28 \'b7\tab Keyboard events\
\'b7\tab Mouse events\
\'b7\tab Tracking-rectangle and cursor-update events\
\'b7\tab Periodic events\
\'b7\tab Other events\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Some of these groups comprise several NSEventType constants, others only one. The following sections discuss the groups, along with the corresponding NSEventType constants.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Keyboard Events\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Among the most common events sent to an application are direct reports of the user\'27s keyboard actions, identified by these NSEventType constants:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab NSKeyDown. The user generated a character or characters by pressing a key.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab NSKeyUp. The key was released.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab NSFlagsChanged. The user pressed or released a modifier key, or turned Alpha Lock on or off.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 Of these, key-down events are the most useful to an application. When a \b type\b0  message returns NSKeyDown, the next step is typically to get the characters generated by the key-down using the \b characters\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Key-up events are used less frequently since they follow almost automatically when there\'27s been a key-down event. And because NSEvent\'27s \b modifierFlags\b0  method returns the state of the modifier keys regardless of the type of event, applications normally don\'27t need to receive flags-changed events; they\'27re useful only for applications that have to keep track of the state of these keys at all times.\
\fs14\
\fs28 For more information on keyboard events, see \'aaKey Events\'ba under the Class Description in the NSResponder class specification.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Mouse Events\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Mouse events are generated by changes in the state of the mouse buttons and by changes in the position of the mouse cursor on the screen. This category consists of:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab NSLeftMouseDown, NSLeftMouseUp, NSRightMouseDown, NSRightMouseUp. \'aaMouse-down\'ba means the user pressed the button; \'aamouse-up\'ba means the user released it. If the mouse has just one button, only left mouse events are generated. By sending a \b clickCount\b0  message to the event, you can determine whether the mouse event was a single click, double click, and so on.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab NSLeftMouseDragged, NSRightMouseDragged. The user moved the mouse with one or more buttons down. NSLeftMouseDragged events are generated when the mouse is moved with its left mouse button down or with both buttons down, and NSRightMouseDragged when it's moved with just the right button down. A mouse with a single button generates only left mouse-dragged events. A series of mouse-dragged events is always preceded by a mouse-down event and followed by a mouse-up event.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab NSMouseMoved. The user moved the mouse without holding down either mouse button. Mouse-moved events are normally not tracked, as they quickly flood the event queue; use NSWindow\'27s \b setAcceptsMouseMovedEvents:\b0  to turn on tracking of mouse movements.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 Mouse-dragged and mouse-moved events are generated repeatedly as long as the user keeps moving the mouse. If the mouse is stationary, neither type of event is generated until the mouse moves again.\
\fs14\
\pard \s46 \li2310 \fi-798 \ri1007 \ql \tx2308 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Note:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 Neither the OpenStep specification nor NeXT\'27s OPENSTEP implementation specifies facilities for the third button of a three-button mouse.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 See \'aaMouse Events\'ba under \'aaEvent Handling\'ba in the NSView class specification for more information on mouse events.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Tracking-Rectangle and Cursor-Update Events\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Because following the mouse\'27s movements precisely is an expensive operation, the Application Kit provides a less intensive mechanism for tracking the location of the mouse. It does this by allowing the application to define regions of the screen, called \i tracking rectangles\i0 , that generate events when the cursor enters or leaves them. The event types are NSMouseEntered and NSMouseExited, and they\'27re generated when the application has asked the Window Server to set a tracking rectangle in a window, typically by using NSView\'27s \b addTrackingRect:owner:userData:assumeInside:\b0  method. A window can have any number of tracking rectangles; NSEvent\'27s \b trackingNumber\b0  method identifies the rectangle that triggered the event.\
\fs14\
\fs28 A special kind of tracking event is the NSCursorUpdate event. This type is used to implement NSView\'27s cursor-rectangle mechanism. An NSCursorUpdate event is generated when the cursor has crossed the boundary of a predefined rectangular area. Applications rarely use NSCursorUpdate events directly, instead using NSView\'27s far more convenient methods.\
\fs14\
\fs28 See \'aaTracking Rectangles and Cursor Rectangles\'ba under \'aaEvent Handling\'ba in the NSView class specification for more information.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Periodic Events\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 An event of type NSPeriodic simply notifies an application that a certain time interval has elapsed. By using the NSEvent class method \b startPeriodicEventsAfterDelay:withPeriod:\b0 , an application can register to receive periodic events and have them placed in its event queue at a certain frequency. When the application no longer needs them, the flow of periodic events can be turned off by invoking \b stopPeriodicEvents\b0 . An application can have only one stream of periodic events active for each thread. Unlike keyboard and mouse events, periodic events aren\'27t dispatched to an NSWindow. The application must retrieve them explicitly using \b nextEventMatchingMask:untilDate:inMode:dequeue:\b0 , typically in a modal loop.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Periodic events are particularly useful in situations where input events aren\'27t generated. For example, when the user holds the mouse down over a scroll button but doesn\'27t move it, no events are generated after the mouse-down event. The scrolling mechanism then has to start and use a stream of periodic events to keep the document scrolling at a reasonable pace until the user releases the mouse. When a mouse-up event occurs, the scrolling mechanism terminates the periodic event stream.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Other Events\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The remaining event types\'d0NSAppKitDefined, NSSystemDefined, and NSApplicationDefined\'d0are less structured, containing only generic subtype and data fields. These three types are extensions to the OpenStep specification, so you shouldn\'27t use them in portable code (periodic events are also implemented in this manner, but are in the specification). Of the three miscellaneous event types, only NSApplicationDefined is of real use to application programs. It allows the application to generate totally custom events and insert them into the event queue. Each such event can have a subtype and two additional codes to different it from others. \b otherEventWithType:...\b0  creates one of these events, and the \b subtype\b0 , \b data1\b0 , and \b data2\b0  methods return the information specific to these events.\
\fs14\
